+/// # It is total over what is stored, and bounded by what it reads
+///
+/// `Gateway::recover` runs this **before the gateway serves anything**, so an `Err` here
+/// spends every *healthy* object's availability on one record — a permanent failure mode
+/// nothing but manual repair exits, which is the cost C-1 does not accept
+/// (`docs/principles.md` §5). Two properties keep that from happening, and both are
+/// load-bearing rather than defensive:
+///
+/// * **The mark comes from the key, so damage cannot lower it.** `inode:<id>` names the id;
+///   the value beside it is never consulted for it. A value that will not decode — #648
+///   enforces the segmented root's structural invariants at decode (ADR-0045), which widens
+///   the set of values that can fail — is therefore attributed and stepped over, never
+///   `?`-propagated. Containing one record's damage never under-approximates the floor the
+///   allocator then trusts, which is the other half of the rule: the two legal answers for a
+///   record a recovery cannot read are *fail closed for that record* or *contribute its true
+///   floor*, never zero.
+/// * **The walk is paged** ([`for_each_page`]), never [`MetadataStore::scan`]: a store merely
+///   *larger* than `SCAN_CAP` stops a gateway from starting exactly as effectively as a
+///   corrupt record does, so a containment rule that held for one and not the other would not
+///   be a containment rule (`crates/traits/src/lib.rs:286`, `:1086-1087`).
+///
+/// Every row the walk cannot fully account for is attributed on [`RECOVERY_AUDIT`] with the
+/// key that names it: a silent skip is the one answer a recovery path must never give, and
+/// the attribution is the repair obligation an operator works from.
+///
+/// # What a page does NOT give: a namespace snapshot — and where that is contained
+///
+/// The mark is a maximum over the keys this walk **saw**, and over a store being written to
+/// concurrently that is not the same as a maximum over the keys that exist.
+/// [`MetadataStore::scan_page`] declines snapshot isolation in the contract itself — "keys
+/// inserted before the cursor after it passed … may be missed …, and **no snapshot isolation
+/// is required of any backend**" (`crates/traits/src/lib.rs:1058-1066`) — and inode keys are
+/// decimal text, so key order is not id order: `inode:100` sorts *before* `inode:99`. A peer
+/// that commits `inode:100` while this cursor is somewhere in the `inode:9…` region therefore
+/// writes **behind** it and is missed. That peer is not hypothetical: it is the legacy,
+/// in-memory-counter gateway of a rolling upgrade, the very deployment the absent
+/// `meta:next_inode` this walk seeds identifies.
+///
+/// Nothing this walk can do closes that, and it is worth stating why rather than reaching for
+/// a stronger read. A second pass would catch the *first* pass's inserts and be open to its
+/// own; the peer never reads the counter this mark seeds, so it keeps allocating after any
+/// walk ends — a floor derived from stored keys is stale the instant it is computed, at any
+/// price in round trips. Requiring a snapshot instead would be a change to the seam's contract
+/// binding all five backends, not to this caller.
+///
+/// So the mark is a **starting point**, and the safety lives at the point of use:
+/// `server::cli::alloc_inode` guards every id it hands out with `require_absent(inode:<id>)`
+/// inside the very commit that reserves it, so an id some record already holds is stepped past
+/// rather than returned. A low floor then costs a few extra compare-and-sets on the first
+/// allocations instead of re-minting a live id — which on the cluster path, where chunk ids are
+/// derived from the inode id and the fragments are written *before* the metadata commit that
