+/// visited because the mark is a *maximum* over ids whose keys are decimal text: `inode:99`
+/// sorts after `inode:100`, so key order is not id order and no bounded "read the largest key"
+/// exists to short-circuit it. Stopping early would answer with a floor an arbitrary distance
+/// below the store's own keys — safe, because the allocator guards each id it hands out (see
